A||||7||theogre||Thermostats revisited
Z||000000||theogre||08-04-99||04:41 PM||||The 180F thermostat i'm playing with is Stant part # 35818 for the 4 cyl. It's available off the shelf almost anyplace. Some store may have to order one. It's not a high demand item.<p>This stat is somewhat diferant in design from the OEM spec Stant 195 one. I think it may flow more water, as the temp diferential between the ECM sensor and the stat value is smaller than the 195's. (The ECM reports 210-215F with a 195 stat.)<p>The 4 cyl ECM is perfectly happy with it's original PROM, the fan on, and the 180 stat in.<p>Ambient Temp, rel humidity: 86F/40% (National Weather service)<p>Engine temp reported to the ECM is 185-193. (checked with AutoXray GM scanner in real time.)<p>Manifold air temp 61-70F. (I don't know where the ECM gets this number.)<p>Engine is in closed loop. (Not in limp mode)<p>I've picked up some power, but w/o a dyno I don't know how much. The car is definatly happy with the change. It's cheap enough. (the 180 was a dollar more than the 195.) <p>I suspect that the V6 should also be fine with the stock prom and a 180 stat, and even the fan switch, but I don't have one to test..... If any of you V6ers are in the Philladelphia area, and want to play, let me know. The only way we'll finally settle these things is to test 'em.
Z||000001||terryk||08-05-99||12:15 AM||||180 is certainly fine, but mileage may suffer a bit. Not enough to matter. It will die with a 160.
Z||000002||Nick||08-05-99||06:58 AM||||My V6 runs much better with a 180.  Now the temp gauge seems to stay put until you sit at idle for about 10 min.  I still have the stock fan switch, so it never turns on, and the car still runs fairly cool.  <p>I ordered an adjustable fan switch that will turn the fan on anywhere from 180 to 240, and when I get it, I plan to play around with different settings to get the best results.  I'm thinking about setting it at 200 to start with, and see how that works.
Z||000003||mwbackus||08-05-99||02:04 PM||||The effects of the 180 stat will most likely only be seen at the speeds necessary to provide air flow thru the radiator so that the stat is actually controlling the temperature.  At idle, the effect is minimal, since the fan switch actually determines the op temp.  In further explanation : at idle, the engine will heat to 180 where upon the stat will open.  Since there is little cooling effect (no airflow thru radiator) the temp will continue to climb until the fan switch comes on and cools it to the fan shut off temperature, a cycle quite simular to running "without" a stat.  At cruise speeds, the stat would control the temp since there would be adequate air flow to provide cooling when the stat opened.  You may find that an open loop condition exists under these conditions (freeway driving, etc.).  I would be very interested in seeing if this is the case, as this has actually been verified by Hypertech's tests with the 160 degree stat (they did not test the 180). It would of course be advantageous to use the 180 stat if closed loop operation is retained under normal driving conditions.  Otherwise, you would see some very negative results (ie..reduced gas mileage, performance, etc) during sustained high speed operation.  The change is not that much lower than the stock stat, so perhaps this would be a viable alternative. <p>Mike
Z||000004||theogre||08-05-99||03:01 PM||||1. my fan is locked on for these tests. Precisely to eliminate the fan switch issue, and because it's been 85-100F outside for over a month. The 235F fan switch is to damn high anyway. The 4 cyl will often start pinging well before that. The fan switch has had allot of play in other threads. <p>2. Engine is running closed loop at all times after warmup. The 87 4 cyl ECM is fast enough to handle scanning in real time while driving. The above numbers were grabbed at a red light, but the numbers haven't varied much wether I'm moving or not.<p>3. A 160 stat would cause a problem in most cars, including many non electronic engines. The design of gasoline engines is generally most efficient at about 190F. This thread wasn't about 160 stats.<p>Nick,<br>does that fan switch go in the block, or on the radiator? Where'd you order it from? If you still have a 195 stat, then 200 is probably a good point for it to come on at. With a 180 I'd set it down more, like 190. That lets the car warm up, and then keeps it stable.
Z||000005||Nick||08-05-99||04:54 PM||||The adjustable temp. fan switch is made by Flex-a-lite, and I ordered it from Summit Racing's website. (part# FLX-31147)<p>I believe it goes into the block, but I'm not sure.  I probably should have looked into it better before ordering it.  Here is the response I got from the guy at Flex-a-lite about whether or not it would work with a stock fan and thermostat.<p>"If it is just a hot and a ground coming from your fan it should work. If it<br>ties in to the computer you would need to know how to wire it in (we do not<br>have info on this due to the fact there are too many computers and the<br>thermostats are made to use with our fans)."<p>From the sounds of it, it will work.  It sounds like it is actually a thermostat, and not just a switch, but maybe not.  Their website describes it as an adjustable temp. thermostat, but Summit's description just says adjustable temp. sensor.  Same thing?  I am not sure this thing will work, so if you know, please let me know.<p>
Z||000006||theogre||08-05-99||05:58 PM||||I can't tell from what info they have posted.
Z||000007||mwbackus||08-06-99||11:56 AM||||Thank you for posting the relevant information about your test, I agree that these conditions rule out the issues I brought up.  Good work!
